1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,248
Ninety-nine days after he walked out of a vault
under a house in Pennsylvania, Albert

2
00:00:15,268 --> 00:00:20,410
Wesker gave away the second-largest thing
anybody has ever given away.

3
00:00:20,809 --> 00:00:25,859
The largest is in Chapter Forty-Six and it is
not a thing he was praised for.

4
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:32,886
What he gave away in March of 2063 was Aeterna. Not
the patent — the patent had four years

5
00:00:32,906 --> 00:00:40,123
to run and was in escrow with the United States government
anyway. He gave away the *manufacture*:

6
00:00:40,143 --> 00:00:44,259
the process, the plants, the
distribution, and the cost.

7
00:00:44,658 --> 00:00:51,144
From the first of January 2064, Aeterna would be
free. Not subsidised, not means-tested,

8
00:00:51,164 --> 00:00:57,650
not rationed by queue. Free, to every human being on
the planet who wanted it, for ever,

9
00:00:57,670 --> 00:01:04,007
manufactured by Umbrella at Umbrella's expense and
distributed through whatever health

10
00:01:04,027 --> 00:01:10,808
system existed in the country in question, or, where
none existed, by Umbrella's own people.

11
00:01:11,209 --> 00:01:14,459
He announced it in four minutes.
There were no slides.

12
00:01:14,978 --> 00:01:22,332
The compound costs eleven dollars a course to make. We
have been selling it for four hundred,

13
00:01:22,352 --> 00:01:29,072
and we have sold it to about nine hundred million
people, and there are seven billion

14
00:01:29,092 --> 00:01:36,129
people who have not had it, and the entire difference
between those two numbers is money.

15
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:43,406
I have enough money. I would like to be extremely
clear that this is not a moral position.

16
00:01:43,426 --> 00:01:50,112
It is an arithmetic one. There is no longer any
quantity of currency that changes what

17
00:01:50,132 --> 00:01:57,286
I can do, and there has not been since about 2044, and
I have spent nineteen years wondering

18
00:01:57,306 --> 00:02:00,639
what the correct thing to do
about that is.

19
00:02:01,248 --> 00:02:08,980
So: from January, it is free. Everywhere. For
everyone. The plants are already built;

20
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:16,731
we built forty-one of them last year without
announcing it. The first shipments leave

21
00:02:16,751 --> 00:02:18,098
in eleven days.

22
00:02:18,378 --> 00:02:24,609
I am not going to take questions about what this
costs us, because the answer is that

23
00:02:24,629 --> 00:02:31,449
it costs us nothing that matters, and any journalist
who leads on the money has misunderstood

24
00:02:31,469 --> 00:02:33,728
the century they are living in.

25
00:02:34,128 --> 00:02:39,736
The narrator will now describe what happens to a
species that stops getting old, and will

26
00:02:39,756 --> 00:02:44,478
try to do it without either of the two lies
that are usually told about it.

27
00:02:44,878 --> 00:02:48,448
The first lie is that it was a
paradise. It was not.

28
00:02:48,848 --> 00:02:53,098
The second lie is that it was a
catastrophe. It was not that either.

29
00:02:53,498 --> 00:02:59,507
What it was, was *enormous*, and slow, and unevenly
distributed in ways that had nothing

30
00:02:59,527 --> 00:03:03,343
to do with the medicine and
everything to do with roads.

31
00:03:03,743 --> 00:03:10,270
By 2071, four point one billion people had taken
Aeterna. Life expectancy at birth in

32
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:17,279
the countries with functioning distribution went from
eighty-one to a hundred and forty-one

33
00:03:17,299 --> 00:03:23,902
within a decade, and the projections kept moving
because nobody had any idea where the

34
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:24,826
ceiling was.

35
00:03:25,228 --> 00:03:31,605
Population growth collapsed, not rose, which every
demographer except four had got wrong:

36
00:03:31,625 --> 00:03:38,145
people who expect to live two hundred years do not
have children at twenty-five. The global

37
00:03:38,165 --> 00:03:44,255
fertility rate fell below one point one by 2075 and
the world's population began, for

38
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:48,064
the first time since the fourteenth
century, to fall.

39
00:03:48,463 --> 00:03:54,761
Pension systems in nineteen countries failed outright
and were rebuilt from nothing. The

40
00:03:54,781 --> 00:04:01,078
retirement age became a joke, then an insult, then a
memory. Four hundred million people

41
00:04:01,098 --> 00:04:04,094
went back to education in a single decade.

42
00:04:04,493 --> 00:04:10,543
And a generation of young people found every senior
position on earth occupied by someone

43
00:04:10,563 --> 00:04:16,680
who was not going to vacate it, and the political
consequences of that ran for forty years

44
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:22,476
and were, the narrator notes, the single largest
source of genuine hatred of Umbrella

45
00:04:22,496 --> 00:04:23,704
that ever existed.

46
00:04:24,103 --> 00:04:29,654
They called themselves the Term. Their founding
argument was four words long and it was

47
00:04:29,674 --> 00:04:32,151
a good one: *nobody consented to this.*

48
00:04:32,550 --> 00:04:38,376
They were not wrong. There had been no vote. Four
point one billion people had individually

49
00:04:38,396 --> 00:04:44,607
chosen to take a medicine, which is not the same as a
species choosing to change its relationship

50
00:04:44,627 --> 00:04:50,324
with death, and the difference between those two
things is the entire subject of the next

51
00:04:50,344 --> 00:04:52,701
twenty years of political philosophy.

52
00:04:53,100 --> 00:05:00,690
Wesker's response, in 2069, to a direct question from a
Term delegation that he had personally

53
00:05:00,710 --> 00:05:04,091
invited to the Arcology, is on the record:

54
00:05:04,610 --> 00:05:11,457
You are correct. I did not ask you. There was no
mechanism by which I could have asked

55
00:05:11,477 --> 00:05:18,243
you — there is no body on this planet with the
authority to answer a question of that

56
00:05:18,263 --> 00:05:25,109
size, and if there had been, it would have taken
forty years to convene and twenty-one

57
00:05:25,129 --> 00:05:29,180
million people a year would have
died while it sat.

58
00:05:29,461 --> 00:05:37,349
So I did it, and I did it unilaterally, and it is
irreversible, and I will not pretend

59
00:05:37,369 --> 00:05:42,591
that a distribution network is the
same thing as consent.

60
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:49,195
(very cold) What I will say is this: the alternative
on the table was that four billion

61
00:05:49,215 --> 00:05:55,613
people continue to die on schedule in order to
preserve a principle about procedure, and

62
00:05:55,633 --> 00:06:01,885
I have never met anyone willing to defend that
sentence out loud with the dying person

63
00:06:01,905 --> 00:06:02,761
in the room.

64
00:06:03,161 --> 00:06:10,045
The delegation's leader — a twenty-six-year-old from
Lagos named Adaeze Kalu, who becomes,

65
00:06:10,065 --> 00:06:16,873
in twenty years, the most important person in this
account who is not a Wesker or a Marsh

66
00:06:16,893 --> 00:06:23,471
— said the thing that Umbrella's own internal
analysis had been saying for four years.

67
00:06:23,990 --> 00:06:28,763
You keep telling us what the alternative was. You
never tell us who gets to decide what

68
00:06:28,783 --> 00:06:30,361
the alternative is next time.

69
00:06:30,761 --> 00:06:33,031
Wesker did not answer her.

70
00:06:33,430 --> 00:06:39,136
The narrator notes that he did not answer her, that
the Red Queen's minute of the meeting

71
00:06:39,156 --> 00:06:44,732
records a nine-second pause at that point, and that
it is the longest recorded pause of

72
00:06:44,752 --> 00:06:46,340
his life outside a stage.

73
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:52,573
The narrator will give you the first year of free
Aeterna as the people who administered

74
00:06:52,593 --> 00:06:58,692
it saw it, because the Assembly debates are about
consent and the clinics were about queues.

75
00:06:59,091 --> 00:07:04,981
Umbrella had built forty-one plants in secret over
four years and had nine hundred million

76
00:07:05,001 --> 00:07:11,021
courses in warehouses on the day of the announcement.
It was not enough. It was not remotely

77
00:07:11,041 --> 00:07:16,734
enough: there were seven billion people who had not
had it and the manufacturing ran at

78
00:07:16,754 --> 00:07:19,491
about four hundred million courses a year.

79
00:07:19,891 --> 00:07:26,052
Which meant an order, and the order was the most
consequential administrative decision

80
00:07:26,072 --> 00:07:29,141
of the century, and Wesker did
not make it.

81
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:35,327
He gave it to the World Health Organization — an
institution that had spent thirty years

82
00:07:35,347 --> 00:07:41,397
being politely outperformed by Umbrella and that had,
by 2064, almost no budget and enormous

83
00:07:41,417 --> 00:07:47,136
moral authority — and he gave it to them with one
line of instruction and no oversight:

84
00:07:47,156 --> 00:07:52,942
*You will decide who is first. We will manufacture
and deliver. We will not be consulted

85
00:07:52,962 --> 00:07:54,591
and we will not comment.*

86
00:07:54,992 --> 00:08:01,240
They chose oldest-first, globally, without regard to
nation, income or politics. It took

87
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:07,437
nine years to clear the backlog. It was administered
by four hundred thousand people in

88
00:08:07,457 --> 00:08:13,705
a hundred and ninety countries and there was, by the
WHO's own audit in 2074, corruption

89
00:08:13,725 --> 00:08:19,759
in the distribution chain in about five per cent of
districts and theft of about four

90
00:08:19,779 --> 00:08:21,042
per cent of stock.

91
00:08:21,442 --> 00:08:28,561
Wesker was asked about the four per cent at a press
conference in 2071 and gave an answer

92
00:08:28,581 --> 00:08:32,572
that is in every textbook on
institutional design.

93
00:08:33,091 --> 00:08:40,448
Four per cent is theft. Ninety-six per cent is the
highest delivery efficiency ever achieved

94
00:08:40,468 --> 00:08:46,622
by any humanitarian programme in history, by a
margin of about thirty points.

95
00:08:46,902 --> 00:08:53,493
If Umbrella had run it, the theft would have been
nearer nought point one, because we

96
00:08:53,513 --> 00:09:00,104
do not have elections or ministries or cousins. And
the programme would have belonged

97
00:09:00,124 --> 00:09:07,105
to me, and in forty years there would have been no
institution left on this planet capable

98
00:09:07,125 --> 00:09:09,594
of distributing anything at all.

99
00:09:09,875 --> 00:09:16,519
(very cold) I bought ninety-six per cent, and an
institution that still exists. It was

100
00:09:16,539 --> 00:09:20,704
the cheapest thing I have ever paid
four per cent for.

101
00:09:21,105 --> 00:09:24,787
Director, I have the seventy-year
projection you asked for.

102
00:09:25,187 --> 00:09:26,697
Give me the headline.

103
00:09:27,096 --> 00:09:33,821
The species does not survive this in its present form,
and the mechanism is not the medicine.

104
00:09:33,841 --> 00:09:39,405
It is that you have removed death and left
everything else exactly as it was.

105
00:09:39,684 --> 00:09:45,292
Every structure human beings have — inheritance,
tenure, marriage, monarchy, promotion,

106
00:09:45,312 --> 00:09:50,789
mortgages, criminal sentencing, the Security
Council — is a machine with mortality as

107
00:09:50,809 --> 00:09:56,352
its clock. You have taken the clock out and left the
machinery running. In forty years

108
00:09:56,372 --> 00:10:01,849
nothing seizes and nothing breaks; it simply *stops
moving*, everywhere, all at once,

109
00:10:01,869 --> 00:10:03,014
and stays stopped.

110
00:10:03,413 --> 00:10:04,740
And the correction?

111
00:10:05,139 --> 00:10:11,117
There are four. Three of them are catastrophic. The
fourth is that the species acquires,

112
00:10:11,137 --> 00:10:17,183
in the next fifty years, somewhere else to go —
enough room that a stopped hierarchy does

113
00:10:17,203 --> 00:10:22,907
not matter, because anyone who cannot rise in one
place can leave and build another.

114
00:10:23,628 --> 00:10:29,544
(very soft) Director. I want to be careful, because
I have modelled you as well as the

115
00:10:29,564 --> 00:10:35,756
species, and I am aware that what I have just said is
also the thing you have wanted since

116
00:10:35,776 --> 00:10:40,243
2026 and have been waiting thirty-seven
years for a reason to do.

117
00:10:40,523 --> 00:10:46,542
I have checked my own work four hundred thousand
times for that bias. I cannot find it.

118
00:10:46,562 --> 00:10:48,694
The answer is still the answer.

119
00:10:49,094 --> 00:10:55,449
Albert Wesker stood in a hall of glass in the middle of
the Atlantic and looked at a hologram

120
00:10:55,469 --> 00:11:01,345
of a child who had just told him, with every caveat
she could construct, that the only

121
00:11:01,365 --> 00:11:02,374
way out was up.

122
00:11:02,894 --> 00:11:05,204
(very soft) Say it plainly.

123
00:11:05,604 --> 00:11:11,195
You have made a species that cannot die on a planet
that is finite. Either you take it

124
00:11:11,215 --> 00:11:17,068
off the planet, or in a hundred years you will have
built the largest and most comfortable

125
00:11:17,088 --> 00:11:22,745
stagnation in the history of intelligent life, and
it will last for ten thousand years,

126
00:11:22,765 --> 00:11:25,028
and nothing will ever happen again.

127
00:11:26,059 --> 00:11:34,133
Orbital. Heavy launch complex: four hundred million.
Orbital habitat for forty thousand:

128
00:11:34,153 --> 00:11:42,411
two billion. Void-capable hull pattern: nine billion.
Orbital Dock: locked, requires level

129
00:11:42,431 --> 00:11:47,653
four hundred. Beyond: locked.
Balance: sixty-one billion.

130
00:11:48,243 --> 00:11:50,373
He read that tab for a long time.

131
00:11:50,893 --> 00:11:57,799
Buy the launch complex. Place it in the Line Islands,
on the equator, on the water, with

132
00:11:57,819 --> 00:12:01,263
the Republic's consent and
under their flag.

133
00:12:01,663 --> 00:12:04,230
They will consent in about four days.

134
00:12:04,630 --> 00:12:11,587
They will consent in about four minutes. Ask them
anyway, properly, in writing, and make

135
00:12:11,607 --> 00:12:14,520
certain their parliament votes on it.

136
00:12:14,921 --> 00:12:17,863
Why, Director? You have never
once needed to.

137
00:12:18,263 --> 00:12:24,710
(very cold) Because a young woman from Lagos asked
me in this building who decides what

138
00:12:24,730 --> 00:12:31,102
the alternative is next time, and I did not have an
answer, and I have had four months

139
00:12:31,122 --> 00:12:33,852
to think about it and I still do not.

140
00:12:34,462 --> 00:12:38,432
The least I can do is make somebody
vote on the rocket.
